ting those effects so one of the  things that we did is decide to combine  those across studies we wind up with 91  total suicidal behaviors and then  whenever you look at those across groups  obviously it wasn't an equal  distribution because we have a treatment  effect 74 and the control group and 17  and the active group and during the  course of that month across all three  studies so what we have so far I think  is extremely promising but I want to  emphasize that these are just the first  steps with these kinds of approaches for  both risk detection and prevention we  still have a ton of work that we want to  do a lot of improvements that we have to  make and right now our future and  ongoing work is really focused on trying  to do those things and make those  improvements but hopefully soon this  kind of work will actually start  translating into changing this century  or longer old trend of suicide death and  suicide attempts and on suicidal  self-injury so thank you all for  listening and thanks very much to my  colleagues who actually made all this  work possible  Thank You dr. Franklin so we have a  couple of questions from the audience  and we just have a couple minutes so  we'll ask a few of them there was one  question about the machine learning  study how did you guys choose the  factors that you decided to finally  include in the algorithm what was your  basis for choosing this the short answer  to that is that the algorithm  essentially chooses them for us and so  we gave it seven hundred and something  to choose from and then it's likely the  ones that were significant and then  something called a cross validation  sample to see if it's significant and  both samples and if it is then it wound  up being included in the model and so  that's one area I didn't have time to  explain but basically the Machine  figures out the optimal algorithm and  configuration for us and was any kind of  family history or anything like that  included in those or were they mainly  medical records that's a great question  what we were really wanting to do with  this initial effort here is create  something that was gonna be replicable  across most system so at Vanderbilt they  have a whole bioinformatics group that  does a lot of this great work and stuff  like natural language processing that  can get a lot more complicated a lot of  genetics work but we wanted this to be  something that and pretty much anyone's  electronic health record and any system  out there that you could just apply this  to that so it was just the intentionally  just the very basic types of diagnosis  and codes and have you so it sounds like  I guess there was a question from the  audience about the generalizability or  the applicable atif or BPD do you think  that this that your treatment  intervention the therapeutic the TEC do  you think that that could be used with  the PD or do you think that there might  be issues with seeing images that might  maybe confound the effects you found  it's a good question I think so we  didn't ask it and particularly in this  study  but I I know just and going back and  forth participants that a lot of them  said that they had been diagnosed with  BPD but certainly we cautioned far and  wide for these kind of studies for what  they're going to see and so we had  people select in the study if they were  already okay with seeing it so it is  unclear what happened but I think  they're a bunch of ways to use these  general types of conditioning principles  to get around that for certain people  well thank you very much thank you all  [Applause]  [Music]
 

